# Git LFS -- the demo video.
#
# trot_video/trot.MOV is 136 MB, and GitHub hard-rejects any file over 100 MB
# on a normal push.  It is one continuous run of walk_demo.py: position crouch,
# torque rise, hold, trot in place, walk out and back.
#
# Before cloning:   git lfs install
# After cloning:    git lfs pull        (or clone with LFS already installed)
#
# Without LFS the clone still works and every offline gate still passes -- the
# working tree just gets a small pointer file where the video should be.
*.MOV  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mov  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp4  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Binary assets that are NOT LFS: small enough to live in git directly, and
# keeping them there means a clone without LFS is still a complete robot model.
*.stl  binary
*.gif  binary

# Line endings.  The control loop runs on a Linux host and the repository is
# edited on Windows, so this is not cosmetic: `t1_preflight.sh` with CRLF fails
# on the robot with `$'\r': command not found`, which is an error message that
# does not name the cause.  Store text as LF, always; check it out as LF too,
# so what an editor sees and what the robot runs are the same bytes.
*          text=auto eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.xml      text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
